digit lead would move this back toward Lean D.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Thom Tillis (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's open Senate seat has become one of the cycle's clearest Democratic pickups. Former Governor Roy Cooper, a strong fundraiser, leads Republican National Committee chair Michael Whatley by about nine to ten points across recent surveys from pollsters of varied affiliations. Expert raters unanimously call the race a Democratic flip, split between Lean D and Likely D, and prediction markets price Cooper's chances around 94-95%.\n\nThe clearest sign of the race's direction came when the top Republican super PAC paused its North Carolina ad spending and shifted money elsewhere, citing Whatley's deficit in polls. Cooper's $20.7 million war chest dwarfs Whatley's $3.5 million, though outside groups have spent heavily attacking him. North Carolina's narrow Republican lean means a late shift could tighten things, but the current evidence points to a comfortable Cooper win.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9425, "margin": 7.94}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.93, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9375, "margin": 8.2}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9346, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9278, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9264, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9322, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.939, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9386, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9577, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.9557, "margin": 9.0}]}